//? Title: Image colorization: lightness-only tensor regression
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Lightness alone cannot uniquely determine an image’s colors. This example predicts Lab chroma while preserving measured lightness,starting with physical color transforms and a hand-checkable ambiguous-color regression. Actual CIFAR images,explicit SGD/Adam fits and lightness-only restored deployment expose the limits of short squared-error training.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.8_other_vision_tasks/image_colorization.cpp`.

> The method

Given normalized lightness L/50-1,the model predicts tanh(a/128,b/128).
An encoder-decoder combines spatial skips and global context,then minimizes
mean squared chroma error. Targets supervise fitting but never enter inference.
Color transforms and metrics retain D65/2degree Lab conventions;RGB clipping
is reserved for display and its declared reconstruction metric.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/image_colorization_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/image_colorization_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/image_colorization_concept.tikz > build/image_colorization_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/image_colorization_concept.svg build/image_colorization_concept.dvi > build/image_colorization_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
